From da2fc5783c22a1f989f93e6716f4b635bf47f55f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rou <shamay1030@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 15:34:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 02/27] Treat FlashInfer workspace overflow as capacity, not
 correctness

The 2026-08-27 formal run lost all 30 trials to one oversized request: a
paged-prefill plan needed 2.92 GB of workspace against 2.68 GB configured, the
fail-closed SparseRepairAttentionError propagated out of ModelRunner.forward
under the default SGLANG_PIC_LEGOLINK_ONLY=1, and the scheduler process died.

Two defects combined.

The split condition did not match the dispatch condition. The attention
dispatcher selects paged prefill from environment alone, but _run_batched_recompute
only split repair rows under flashinfer_full_row_batching, which additionally
requires request-level row batching. With -AllowRequestRowBatchingOverride a
request could turn row batching off, skip the split, and still reach paged
prefill with a single 6,276-token row. Splitting is now gated on
paged_attention_path_reachable(), which mirrors the dispatcher exactly.

Capacity was handled like a correctness failure. Native sparse-repair attention
computes the same logical mask and is the reference the correctness suite
compares against, so a workspace overflow can be answered by degrading the
operator for one request without weakening any LegoLink claim. Falling back to
full prefill would forfeit the reuse claim and stays fail-closed.

- SparseRepairCapacityError subclasses SparseRepairAttentionError, so existing
  handlers still catch it; _is_capacity_exhaustion matches CUDA OOM by type and
  the FlashInfer C++ allocator by message.
- SGLANG_PIC_FLASHINFER_CAPACITY_POLICY selects adaptive (default) or strict.
  Non-capacity failures fail closed under both.
- PagedBatchCapacityMemo bisects a per-shape ceiling rather than predicting
  FlashInfer's version-dependent batch_prefill_tmp_v formula: a failure halves
  the ceiling, a success raises a floor the ceiling cannot drop below, and the
  ceiling never collapses below 64 tokens. One request pays for the discovery
  and later requests pre-split.
- A degraded request keeps every remaining layer on native so one request never
  mixes attention operators.
- Capacity state is recorded in metadata and the batch-plan trace, so a run that
  degraded is distinguishable from one that did not.

Block pins were never leaked; _guard_legolink_release already released them in a
finally and is unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
